Now I have the full test file. This is an MSTest 4.1.0 integration test class (`.NET 10`, `[TestCategory("Integration")]`). Let me deliver the formal 19-smell audit.

---

## testsmells.org 19-Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · **.NET 10** · **Integration-test calibration applied** (class bears `[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

---

### 1 · Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High | 3 | 3 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4 (of 7)** |

---

### 2 · Findings by Severity

---

#### ❶ Conditional Test Logic — **High**

**Taxonomy:** testsmells.org — *Conditional Test Logic*
**Affected:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101-108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Problem:** The `if/else` branch means only *one* path ever executes per run. If the repository always returns a base `User`, the `PremiumUser` branch is dead code that silently provides zero coverage. Even for integration tests, this hides a testing gap — you can never be sure which assertion ran without inspecting the data.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is silently never tested; a regression in that path will not be caught.

---

#### ❷ Sleepy Test — **High**

**Taxonomy:** testsmells.org — *Sleepy Test*
**Affected:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);
```

**Problem:** A hard 3-second wall-clock sleep makes this test non-deterministic (may fail on slow CI) and unconditionally slows the suite. The method is already declared `async Task` yet uses a *synchronous* `Thread.Sleep`, blocking the thread pool. Even under integration-test calibration, a fixed sleep is a smell — polling or event-based waiting is preferred.

**Fix — poll with a timeout:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures when the system is slow; unconditional 3 s added to every run even when the event fires in < 100 ms.

---

#### ❸ Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test) — **High**

**Taxonomy:** testsmells.org — *Unknown Test / Empty Test*
**Affected:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113-119)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

**Problem:** No assertion is present. MSTest will report this as **passed** even if `Insert` silently drops every row or corrupts data — only an unhandled exception would fail it. The name `RunsWithoutErrors` signals a "does-not-throw" intent, but that is implicit and leaves functional correctness entirely unverified.

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — the test always passes, even if data is silently lost.

---

#### ❹ Assertion Roulette — **Medium**

**Taxonomy:** testsmells.org — *Assertion Roulette*
**Affected:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35-38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Problem:** Four assertions, none with a message parameter. When one fails in CI, the log shows a bare `Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<…>` with no indication of *which property* was wrong without reading the stack trace line number.

**Fix — add message parameters:**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage when a failure occurs in CI — developers must locate the exact line to understand which property failed.

---

### 3 · Smell-Free Patterns (Well-Written Tests)

The remaining **3 tests** are clean and well-structured:

| Test | Notes |
|------|-------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear arrange-act-assert, focused behavior, 2 assertions with obvious intent. |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Minimal, single `Assert.IsNull` verifies absence — excellent. |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await, single count assertion contextually obvious (10 tasks → 10 rows). |

`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` is also clean — `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` is **not** a Magic Number because 3 items were explicitly inserted on the preceding lines (contextual calibration applied).

---

### 4 · Smells Not Detected (15 of 19)

The following smells from the full taxonomy were checked and **not found**:

Mystery Guest, Eager Test, Magic Number Test, Sensitive Equality, Exception Handling, General Fixture, Ignored/Disabled Test, Duplicate Assert, Lazy Test, Constructor Initialization, Default Test, Redundant Print, Redundant Assertion, Resource Optimism, Empty Test.

Notably, the use of **in-memory SQLite** (`Data Source=:memory:`) is hermetic and does *not* constitute a Mystery Guest — the database is created and destroyed per test with no external dependency.

---

### 5 · Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Impact |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| **1** | Assertion-Free Test (`BulkInsert`) | Add `Assert.AreEqual(100, …)` | Eliminates false-pass risk |
| **2** | Conditional Test Logic (`GetUser`) | Split into 2 focused tests | Guarantees both branches are tested |
| **3** | Sleepy Test (`NotifyOnInsert`) | Replace `Thread.Sleep` with poll loop | Removes flakiness + 3 s wall-clock |
| **4** | Assertion Roulette (`InsertUser`) | Add message strings to 4 assertions | Faster CI failure triage |

**Bottom line:** 3 of 7 tests have high-severity smells that risk false passes or flaky failures. The fixes are surgical — each is a few lines. The remaining 4 tests (including `ListUsers` and the concurrent-insert test) are well-designed for integration scope.
